ing, and other evaluations; the combined results guide clinical decision-making and inform treatment planning. This entity is directly connected to creation of a treatment plan, underscoring that staging is operationally tied to care choices. [Data: Entities (4646); Relationships (10022)]\\n\\nHigh-resolution anoscopy is specifically referenced as a procedure within the diagnostic workup, indicating availability of specialized diagnostic tooling within this care pathway. [Data: Relationships (14541)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Specialist involvement is emphasized: oncology teams with high case experience and colorectal surgeons for evaluation\",\"explanation\":\"The oncology team is recommended for managing anal cancer, with the text highlighting the benefit of finding a team experienced in treating many anal cancer cases per year due to the cancer’s rarity. This elevates provider selection as a core community theme and implies that outcomes and care quality may be sensitive to provider experience. [Data: Entities (6218); Relationships (14273)]\\n\\nColorectal surgeons are described as specialists best qualified to evaluate suspected anal cancer, and evaluation by a health care professional is emphasized for symptoms suggestive of anal cancer. Together, these relationships describe a referral/escalation model from general clinical evaluation to specialty assessment. [Data: Entities (6034, 6035); Relationships (13780, 13784)]\\n\\nA survivor voice (Melissa) reinforces the expertise theme by advising patients to seek an oncology team that treats many cases annually because of the disease’s rarity. [Data: Entities (6212); Relationships (14309)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Anatomical entities (anal canal, perianal region, anal opening) frame where disease arises and how it is examined\",\"explanation\":\"The anal canal is described as a short passage connecting the rectum to the anal opening, surrounded by anal sphincter muscles, and it is identified as a primary site where anal cancer can originate. It is also routinely inspected during anoscopy/HRA, where acetic acid and magnification may be used to identify abnormal tissue for biopsy. [Data: Entities (6003, 6005); Relationships (13696)]\\n\\nThe perianal region is defined as including the anal opening and surrounding skin, and anal cancer can originate in this region as well as in the anal canal. The perianal region can be staged using the TNM system and may develop precancerous lesions relevant to anal/perianal malignancies, positioning anatomy as essential for staging and treatment selection (e.g., local excision vs chemoradiation as described for perianal cancer). [Data: Entities (6004, 6107); Relationships (13699, 13968)]\\n\\nThe broader “anal region” is linked to anal cancer as the anatomical focus of care, reinforcing that multiple adjacent anatomical subregions are clinically relevant within the same disease umbrella. [Data: Entities (6391); Relationships (14637)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Staging categories (Stage I; Stage III/IIIA) and lymphatic pathways highlight progression concerns\",\"explanation\":\"Stage I anal cancer is defined by tumor size (2 cm or smaller, T1) with no lymph node spread (N0) and no distant metastasis, indicating early-stage localized disease. Stage III anal cancer is described as involving substages and potentially nearby lymph node involvement; Stage IIIA is referenced with tumor size criteria and potential nearby lymph node involvement. These staging entities provide a structured framework for prognosis and treatment decisions. [Data: Entities (6076, 6078, 6093); Relationships (13863, 13897)]\\n\\nThe lymphatics are described as pathways through which anal cancer cells can travel to lymph nodes in the groin and pelvis if untreated, aligning with the staging emphasis on nodal involvement and the need for appropriate workup. [Data: Entities (6063); Relationships (13769)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Symptom presentation and misattribution risk: rectal bleeding vs hemorrhoids\",\"